;;; An activator a rule derives binds a relation parameter to a fact, and the
;;; run stops where its plan is compiled — M1f **W59**, register row
;;; `W59/derived-activator` (`corpus/wrong_answers.toml`), filed by S1f.5.18
;;; (T1f.5.18.1, 2026-11-09), `open` and owned by S1f.5.18, on the user's
;;; ruling of 2026-11-08 on Q-M1f.1 (*"Q1 - b"*). The program is S1f.5.5's
;;; probe `g14/derived-activator.ein`, `:why`s added.
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; A rule `r` whose parameter `?R` names a relation, `(box (?R b))`, and a
;;; rule `mk` that derives `r`'s activator from `item`'s argument, `(r ?f)`.
;;; `item` is a declared relation, so its argument holds any value, a fact
;;; among them, and `(item (a b))` is one: `mk` derives `(r (a b))`, which
;;; binds `?R` to the fact `(a b)`.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; The ruling (Q-M1f.1 (b)): a program in which some rule could derive an
;;; activator binding a relation parameter to a fact is refused at load, by a
;;; static check, whether or not a run derives it. `mk` writes `(r ?f)`, `?f`
;;; is `r`'s relation parameter `?R`, and `(item ?f)` can bind `?f` to a fact,
;;; so every command that loads the program exits 1 with the loader's line
;;; (`ein test`: 2).
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answers ────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; The program loads, root fires `mk` and derives `(r (a b))`, and the run
;;; stops where `r`'s plan is compiled, exit 1, with W24's line, *the relation
;;; head ?R of (?R b) at Loc(…) is bound to the fact (a b) — a relation is
;;; named by a symbol, not a fact.*, on every command (`ein solve FILE` at
;;; `8249e872`, release and debug alike). Without `(item (a b))` it answers:
;;; `derived-activator-never.ein`, W59's other row.
;;;
;;; ── Since M1f S1f.5.18 ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; The loader refuses the program (T1f.5.18.3, 2026-11-09): every command
;;; that loads it exits 1 with one line naming `mk`, `r` and `?R` (`ein
;;; test`: 2), in either build, where it stopped where `r`'s plan was compiled, W24's line. The register's `now` is that
;;; refusal; the section above is the record of the answer it replaced.

(relation box Node :why "{?1} is in the box")
(relation hit Node :why "{?1} is hit")
(relation item Node :why "{?1} is an item")
(box (a b))
(rule r (?R)
  :match (box (?R b))
  :assert (hit z)
  :why "a box holds a {?R} of b")
(rule mk ()
  :match (item ?f)
  :assert (r ?f)
  :why "{?f} is an item, so r is asked of it")
(item (a b))
(query :goal (hit ?x))
